          "p": "He was born René Descartes on 31 March 1596, in a small town in the Touraine, in central France, between Tours and Châtellerault. The town is now simply called *Descartes*; it was renamed in his honor, first in 1802 and finally in 1967. (The Latin form of his name, *Renatus Cartesius*, is why his philosophy is called \"Cartesian\" and his coordinate grid the \"Cartesian plane.\") His mother died when he was barely a year old, and he was a sickly child. He went at about eleven to the Jesuit Collège de La Flèche, among the best schools in Europe, where he was taught the humanist and Aristotelian-Scholastic curriculum at its finest — and where, by his own later account, he formed two convictions that would drive his whole life. The first was admiration: the mathematics he learned there was genuinely solid, certain in a way nothing else was, and he never lost his taste for it. The second was dissatisfaction: for all the centuries of accumulated learning, the deepest questions remained exactly as unsettled as ever, with the learned still disagreeing about everything that mattered. He came out of the best education on the continent convinced that none of it rested on anything firm."
